// Runtime semantic-correctness smoke-test for the Phase 2a ownership-
// drop-ledger track (fix/ownership-drop-ledger, 0.31.0).  Companion
// to the MIR-level emission pin in
// `lang/tests/stage2/test_match_scrut_copy_store_emits_copyvalue.py`,
// which is the authoritative regression gate for the
// `match Optional<String>` double-drop UAF.
//
// This fixture exercises the full compile+run+drop pipeline for a
// refcount-bearing variant match: `Optional<Wrapper>` where Wrapper
// is a user struct with an explicit `impl core.Copy` over a String
// field — the same type-shape the TLS team's `_base_port()` helper
// created at runtime (via `std.env::get` under a packaged stdlib
// load).  Verifying this fixture runs clean under valgrind-memcheck
// proves that the binder-level field retains, the match-arm
// scrut_tmp accounting, and the scope-exit drop symmetry are all
// correct for the refcounted-payload case.
//
// Scope caveat (important for reviewers): in source-compile mode,
// the Drift trait system resolves
// `implement<T> Copy for Optional<T> require T is Copy` LESS eagerly
// than the packaged-`.dmp`-load path.  As a result
// `_should_copy_value(Optional<Wrapper>)` returns False here and the
// scrut goes down the MoveOut branch of `_ensure_arm_scrut_ptr`, NOT
// the Copy-store else-branch that was the original bug site.  So
// this fixture does NOT strictly reproduce the UAF pre-Phase-2a — it
// would pass even against a buggy compiler.  The companion stage2
// unit test uses the packaged-load Copy-query hook to force the bug-
// shape classification and pin the MIR emission directly, which IS
// the strict regression gate.  The authoritative runtime proof is
// the TLS team's downstream e2e run against a freshly-deployed
// toolchain (Gate C in the 0.31.0 history entry).
//
// What this fixture catches: regressions in the MoveOut branch's
// binder-level retain path, struct-field copy insertion, or scope-
// exit drop ordering for a refcount-bearing payload — any of which
// would surface as exit != 5 OR non-zero valgrind errors.
//
// The test asserts exit code 5 (length of "hello") and is verified under
// valgrind-memcheck in the runner config (see
// `test_run_gate_a_under_valgrind` — Phase 2c adds that harness).  If the
// UAF returns, the native exit would be 134 (SIGABRT) or the binary
// would print `tcache_thread_shutdown` to stderr on process exit.

module main;

import std.core as core;

pub struct Wrapper {
	pub inner: String
}

implement core.Copy for Wrapper {}

fn _make() nothrow -> Optional<Wrapper> {
	return Optional::Some(Wrapper(inner = "hello"));
}

fn main() nothrow -> Int {
	val w = _make();
	match w {
		Optional::Some(x) => {
			return x.inner.byte_length();
		},
		Optional::None() => {
			return 0;
		},
	}
}
